00:00:50.418 --> 00:00:53.546
- I'm gonna jump right in, and give everybody

00:00:53.546 --> 00:00:55.170
a little background about tuberculosis.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.930
Obviously TB's been with us forever.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:02.185
We've read about it in books from thousands of years ago.

00:01:02.185 --> 00:01:03.240
- Egyptian mummies.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.370
- Egyptian mummies, that's right.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:13.380
And yet here we are 21st century, and it's still with us.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.910
Have we been making progress?

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.940
Give us a little state of play of TB.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.560
Where are we with TB?

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.519
How much progress have we made?

00:01:21.519 --> 00:01:23.310
What's going on with tuberculosis globally?

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.530
- Really important issue.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:29.395
Whenever I speak about the progress in TB

00:01:29.395 --> 00:01:34.560
I show a slide of my grandfather in India, my mother's father.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.900
Even my mother has no memory of him because he died in his 20s of TB.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.860
Now I'm talking about 80 years ago, and we

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.130
know that there was nothing much anybody could have offered him.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.580
Born in the wrong era, died so we could live with that.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.840
But to have 1.5 million people dying of TB today

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.010
is just nuts because it's a curable bacterial infection.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:02.790
And to have that many people dying even today

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:07.200
basically suggests that something is seriously off in how tbs managed.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:07.780
- Yeah.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.300
- There is no rush now to lose someone because we have the antibiotics.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.260
The antibiotics are bloody cheap.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.000
There is really no reason to lose someone to TB today.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.310
A few people with severe drug resistant TB that I understand,

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.730
but most people are not dying of drug resistant TB.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:24.980
- That's what I was gonna say.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.960
Is the problem primarily now that everybody

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.330
who's dying, everybody who's really sick just

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.250
has really highly drug resistant TB?

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.480
Is that a major problem?

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.740
- No, no, not at all.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.620
Drug resistant TB is a problem no doubt, but it's

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.641
mostly localized in some parts of the world.

00:02:39.641 --> 00:02:40.140
- Got it.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.680
- And the majority of deaths are not happening in drug resistant cases.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.110
Numerically the numbers are relatively smaller.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:49.320
From one point to eight millions are not drug resistant.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.690
Most of them are drug sensitive TB.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.820
They have somehow been botched up.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.930
They have either received care too late, or they not received care at all,

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:02.640
or they've been just mismanaged in the various sectors of health care.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.210
The mortality is staggeringly high, and today

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:13.080
actually, the WHO says TB has surpassed the death burden of HIV and malaria.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.965
In other words, TB kills more people today than HIV and malaria combined.

00:03:16.965 --> 00:03:17.730
- Wow.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.100
- Which is the most recent statistics.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.240
And it ranks in the top 10 causes of deaths in the global burden of disease

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.890
even today, in the 2015 data.

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:31.320
- Let me take a quick step back, and just unpack that a little bit.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.640
One question that I was going to ask you is, how does TB

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.140
compare to these other big killers?

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.320
Malaria, HIV aids, sounds like we've made real progress

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.780
with HIV aids, and malaria.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.090
Are we making progress?

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.620
Is TB getting better, but maybe just not getting better as fast?

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:48.330
Where it that at?

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:51.510
- The latest projections shows that in the last 15 years

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.470
TB deaths have declined by about 20%.

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:55.180
That's not bad.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:55.680
- Yeah.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.120
- But you still are left with a residual 1.8 million deaths a year.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.410
Which means that for significant worse to start with.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:02.910
- Yeah.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:08.490
- Secondly, the numeric numbers of TB new cases have gone up.

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.590
The year before it was nine plus million.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:15.761
The last world TB report, 2016, WHO increased it to 10.4.

00:04:15.761 --> 00:04:16.260
- Wow.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:17.700
- That's today's number.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:21.870
10.4 million cases, and 1.8 million deaths.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:23.530
- Wow, every year?

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:24.660
- Every year.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.350
Every year new cases of TB every year, and almost half

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:32.160
a million drug resistant cases a year is the current estimates.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:32.850
- Got it.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:37.680
- In some ways numerically it's worsened compared to previous years.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.970
Which is the opposite of what most infectious diseases are trending to,

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.900
and part of it is the fact that the numbers went up because of India.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:49.020
It's not that India has suddenly discovered TB.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:52.530
The private sector in India is starting to notify which as you know

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.540
is a whole another issue with TB.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.900
When the private sector does not even tell you what they're are managing you

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:02.040
end up with this hole in the data set.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:07.190
The honest answer is nobody in the world truly knows the true magnitude of TB.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:09.020
Especially in countries like India.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:13.730
There's a whole slew of non docs providers who are managing TB.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:16.640
Who may not be telling anyone that they're managing TB.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:19.090
When they start notifying the numbers jump up.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:20.360
That's what happened in India.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:21.810
- Got it.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:25.170
10.5 million is almost surely an underestimate.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:26.990
- Almost surely an estimate.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:30.982
- And the numbers have gone up not because TB is number of TB cases

00:05:30.982 --> 00:05:32.690
is exploding, but we're actually starting

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:36.235
to identify, and capture cases that have been there before,

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:37.610
but we just didn't know about it.

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:39.200
- We didn't know about it.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.510
That's even true for drug resistance.

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:45.020
All of these decades we we're underestimating drug resistance

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:46.520
because we never even tested for it.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:50.340
We we're using sputum smears which cannot detect drug resistance.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:53.450
That was the dominant diagnostic tool for TB.

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:56.270
Now that we are starting to use molecular test like gene export,

00:05:56.270 --> 00:06:00.530
suddenly we are seeing much more [INAUDIBLE] percent in MDR resistance.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.870
Which again, I think is not because something suddenly

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:06.500
has exploded because we are more aware, we are looking for it now.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:07.400
- Got it.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.640
Both the burden, and the loss of life is not primarily

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.021
due to the underlying disease itself.

00:06:13.021 --> 00:06:15.020
The underlying disease of course is problematic,

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:16.115
but we know how to manage it.

00:06:16.115 --> 00:06:17.240
- We know how to manage it.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:21.210
- And the second is that it's not that we have no strategy for coverage.

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:24.770
It's not that we have no ability to get people into care.

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:28.620
That doesn't strike me as the dominant problem.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.010
And what I'm hearing is that the dominant problem really

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:34.940
is in the execution of what we do, and how comprehensively,

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:36.830
